load ../actsfor-unanimity.maude

--- Fixture discipline: two authorities, three principals, and each fixture says
--- exactly which edges each authority declares. `ask(X, Y)` asks whether the
--- composition lets X act for Y.

--- Coverage: an edge both authorities declare survives the meet.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-ACTSFOR-UNANIMITY :
  composition(env(a1, e(p, q)) + env(a2, e(p, q)))
  ask(p, q)
  =>* C:Cfg composedReaches(p, q) .

--- Coverage: and so does a chain, when both declare both links. Transitivity is
--- not what unanimity takes away.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-ACTSFOR-UNANIMITY :
  composition(env(a1, e(p, q) ; e(q, r)) + env(a2, e(p, q) ; e(q, r)))
  ask(p, r)
  =>* C:Cfg composedReaches(p, r) .

--- Bite: an edge only one authority declares does not survive. This is the case
--- §2 permits at authoring time — a1 writing an edge that claims a2's principal
--- — and it is unanimity, not the authoring rule, that makes it inert.
--- No solution.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-ACTSFOR-UNANIMITY :
  composition(env(a1, e(p, q)) + env(a2, mtEdge))
  ask(p, q)
  =>* composedReaches(p, q) RESIDUAL:Cfg .

--- THE HEADLINE, ACTSFOR_MEET_IS_WEAKER. a1 declares p→q and a2 declares q→r:
--- each has half a chain and neither reaches r from p. No reachable state holds
--- the composed reach together with a constituent that lacks it, so closure over
--- the intersection stays inside the intersection of the closures.
--- No solution.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-ACTSFOR-UNANIMITY :
  composition(env(a1, e(p, q)) + env(a2, e(q, r)))
  ask(p, r)
  =>* composedReaches(p, r) constituentLacks(A:Auth, p, r) RESIDUAL:Cfg .

--- Tooth: the same fixture with the edges pooled. The closure now walks a1's
--- p→q into a2's q→r and reaches r from p — a delegation neither authority
--- granted, assembled out of two halves that were never meant to meet.
search [1] in WHIPPLESCRIPT-ACTSFOR-POOLED-EDGES :
  composition(env(a1, e(p, q)) + env(a2, e(q, r)))
  ask(p, r)
  =>* C:Cfg composedReaches(p, r) constituentLacks(A:Auth, p, r) .
